{{also|morgen}}
==German==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈmɔʁɡən/|[ˈmɔʁ.ɡ(ə)n]|[ˈmɔʁ.ɡŋ̍]|[ˈmɔɐ̯-]|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-Morgen.ogg|audio|lang=de}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{m|gmh|morgen}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|morgan}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*murganaz}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*mr̥Hko||to blink, twinkle}}. Compare Low German {{m|nds|Morgen}}, Dutch {{m|nl|morgen}}, West Frisian {{m|fy|moarn}}, English {{m|en|morn}}, {{m|en|morrow}}, Danish {{m|da|morgen}}, Swedish {{m|sv|morgon}}.

====Noun====
{{de-noun|m|Morgens|Morgen|pl2=Morgende}}

# {{l|en|morning}}
# {{lb|de|historical}} {{l|en|morgen}} {{gloss|measure of land}}
# {{lb|de|archaic|poetic}} {{l|en|east}}
#: {{ux|de|gen '''Morgen''' gehen|walk in the direction where the sun rises}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The normal plural is unchanged '''''Morgen'''''. The plural {{m|de|Morgende}} is of dialectal origin. It is rather common colloquially but hardly ever used in literary German.
* ''Morgen'' includes the whole time of day between sunrise and noon, though the time roughly between 9 a.m. and noon is often specified as {{m|de|Vormittag}}.

